g_level values: {condition, value, unit_symbol}. So value here is a number or string; for \"2 × 5000\" keep string \"2 × 5000\".\n\nWait — the schema says values follow the structured form {condition, value, unit_symbol}. But earlier rule 2 says every characteristic value must be object with value/unit_symbol/footnote_markers. For config_level, the items are {condition, value, unit_symbol} where value itself is the raw value (number|string). Hmm, looking at rule 2 more strictly: \"Every characteristic value MUST be an object with this shape\" — but that applies to the leaf values in type_level. For config_level, the schema shows {condition, value}. I think for config_level entries, each item is {condition, value, unit_symbol} where value is number|string|{min,max}. Let me follow that.\n\nActually wait, should config_level values be nested like {\"condition\": \"single_interval\", \"value\": {\"value\": 10000, \"unit_symbol\": null}}? That's double nesting, seems redundant. The schema says: `attribute, axis, values[]: {condition, value}`. So value is direct. I'll do that.\n\n- maximum_tare_effect: \"−Max\" → value \"-Max\" string.\n- operating_temperature_range: −10 °C to +40 °C → {min: -10, max: 40}, unit_symbol \"°C\".\n- power supply: \"12 VDC external supply (100–240 VAC, 50/60 Hz); 6 VDC rechargeable battery\". Split:\n  - external_supply_voltage: 12, unit \"V\" (DC) — attribute \"external_power_supply_voltage\", value 12, unit_symbol \"V\".\n  - mains_voltage: {min:100, max:240}, unit \"V\" (AC).\n  - mains_frequency: {min:50, max:60}, unit \"Hz\".\n  - battery_voltage: 6, unit \"V\".\n  Rule 3 example: `110-240V AC 50/60Hz` → split into power_supply_voltage, power_supply_frequency, power_supply_type. Here we have both 12 VDC external and 6 VDC battery, plus 100-240 VAC. So:\n  - \"external_power_supply_voltage\": {value: 12, unit_symbol: \"V\"} — DC. Maybe add power_supply_type entries. I could add \"external_power_supply_type\": \"DC\", \"mains_power_supply_t